1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,882
Welcome to episode eight.

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:06,265
So far we've calculated the
energy a fuel should release.

3
00:00:06,305 --> 00:00:11,417
Now we measure the energy it actually
delivers to something useful,

4
00:00:11,457 --> 00:00:14,609
like a can of water, and compare the two.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,137
Two quick checks.

6
00:00:16,177 --> 00:00:21,742
Ethanol's molar enthalpy of combustion is
negative one thousand three hundred and

7
00:00:21,782 --> 00:00:23,680
seventy kilojoules per mole.

8
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:28,871
How much energy is released when zero
point zero two zero zero moles burns?

9
00:00:28,911 --> 00:00:34,546
And what is seventeen thousand nine hundred
and seventy-four joules in kilojoules?

10
00:00:34,586 --> 00:00:35,762
Pause and answer.

11
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:49,554
Zero point zero two zero zero times one
thousand three hundred and seventy gives

12
00:00:49,594 --> 00:00:52,520
twenty-seven point four
kilojoules released.

13
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:58,116
And seventeen thousand nine hundred and
seventy-four joules is seventeen point nine

14
00:00:58,156 --> 00:00:59,600
seven four kilojoules.

15
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:03,954
Hold on to both: they're about to
appear in a practice question.

16
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:09,303
Here's a common way to measure
the energy from a fuel.

17
00:01:09,343 --> 00:01:12,407
A spirit burner holds the liquid fuel.

18
00:01:12,447 --> 00:01:16,736
Above it, a metal can holds
a measured mass of water,

19
00:01:16,776 --> 00:01:20,370
with a thermometer to
track its temperature.

20
00:01:20,733 --> 00:01:25,688
When the fuel burns, the energy
it releases goes several places.

21
00:01:25,728 --> 00:01:30,528
Some heats the water: that's the
useful energy we can measure.

22
00:01:30,568 --> 00:01:33,572
Some heats the can and the thermometer.

23
00:01:33,612 --> 00:01:36,928
And a lot simply heats
the surrounding air,

24
00:01:36,968 --> 00:01:41,495
especially the hot gases rising
past the sides of the can.

25
00:01:41,866 --> 00:01:43,619
There's another loss too.

26
00:01:43,659 --> 00:01:47,133
In a small flame, combustion
is often incomplete.

27
00:01:47,173 --> 00:01:50,145
You may see soot on the bottom of the can.

28
00:01:50,185 --> 00:01:55,595
Incompletely burned fuel releases less
energy than the equation for complete

29
00:01:55,635 --> 00:01:57,069
combustion predicts.

30
00:01:57,466 --> 00:02:04,062
So the energy gained by the water is
always less than the energy the fuel could

31
00:02:04,102 --> 00:02:04,734
release.

32
00:02:04,774 --> 00:02:10,654
Our job is to measure that useful
part, and compare it with the total.

33
00:02:11,599 --> 00:02:12,540
Start small.

34
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:19,247
To warm one gram of water by one degree
Celsius takes four point one eight joules.

35
00:02:19,287 --> 00:02:23,663
That number is the specific
heat capacity of water, c:

36
00:02:23,703 --> 00:02:27,629
four point one eight joules
per gram per degree.

37
00:02:27,999 --> 00:02:33,174
Two grams need twice as much energy
for the same one-degree rise,

38
00:02:33,214 --> 00:02:39,031
and two hundred and fifty grams need
two hundred and fifty times as much.

39
00:02:39,071 --> 00:02:42,841
So energy is proportional
to the mass of water.

40
00:02:43,199 --> 00:02:49,628
And warming the same water by ten degrees
instead of one takes ten times the energy.

41
00:02:49,668 --> 00:02:53,903
So energy is also proportional
to the temperature rise.

42
00:02:54,265 --> 00:02:58,247
Put those together: q equals
m times c times delta T.

43
00:02:58,287 --> 00:03:03,711
Check the units: grams, times joules
per gram per degree, times degrees.

44
00:03:03,751 --> 00:03:07,165
Grams cancel, degrees
cancel, leaving joules.

45
00:03:07,532 --> 00:03:09,535
Now the important questions.

46
00:03:09,575 --> 00:03:10,338
Whose mass?

47
00:03:10,378 --> 00:03:15,300
The water's, because the water is
what's being heated, not the fuel.

48
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:19,459
And delta T is the temperature
rise, final minus initial,

49
00:03:19,499 --> 00:03:21,907
not the final temperature itself.

50
00:03:22,864 --> 00:03:26,655
Rearranging gives delta T
equals q divided by m c.

51
00:03:26,695 --> 00:03:28,878
Let's see what that predicts.

52
00:03:28,918 --> 00:03:35,008
Put four thousand one hundred and eighty
joules into one hundred grams of water:

53
00:03:35,048 --> 00:03:37,653
the temperature rises ten degrees.

54
00:03:37,997 --> 00:03:43,691
Put the same energy into two hundred
grams of water: the rise halves,

55
00:03:43,731 --> 00:03:45,020
to five degrees.

56
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:48,882
The energy is spread over
twice as much water.

57
00:03:49,264 --> 00:03:54,656
Now double the energy, to eight thousand
three hundred and sixty joules,

58
00:03:54,696 --> 00:04:00,164
into the original one hundred grams:
the rise doubles, to twenty degrees.

59
00:04:00,204 --> 00:04:06,425
Keep these two effects in mind whenever a
question changes the mass of water or the

60
00:04:06,465 --> 00:04:07,597
amount of fuel.

61
00:04:08,529 --> 00:04:14,115
To know how much fuel burned, weigh
the burner before and after heating.

62
00:04:14,155 --> 00:04:19,741
The mass lost is the mass of fuel
burned, assuming nothing else escaped.

63
00:04:19,781 --> 00:04:23,491
Divide by molar mass to
get the amount in moles,

64
00:04:23,531 --> 00:04:29,429
then multiply by the molar enthalpy of
combustion to get the energy the fuel

65
00:04:29,469 --> 00:04:30,269
released.

66
00:04:30,529 --> 00:04:33,379
The same idea measures energy in food.

67
00:04:33,419 --> 00:04:39,693
Suppose one point five zero grams of dry
food burns under a can holding one hundred

68
00:04:39,733 --> 00:04:45,397
point zero grams of water, and the
water warms by eight point zero degrees.

69
00:04:45,437 --> 00:04:51,330
The water gains one hundred times four
point one eight times eight point zero:

70
00:04:51,370 --> 00:04:55,249
three thousand three hundred
and forty-four joules.

71
00:04:55,596 --> 00:05:01,557
Per gram of food, that's three thousand
three hundred and forty-four divided by one

72
00:05:01,597 --> 00:05:05,678
point five zero: about two
point two kilojoules per gram.

73
00:05:05,718 --> 00:05:11,607
That's far less than a typical nutrition
label value of fifteen or more kilojoules

74
00:05:11,647 --> 00:05:14,757
per gram for a dry,
carbohydrate-rich food.

75
00:05:15,129 --> 00:05:16,022
Two reasons.

76
00:05:16,062 --> 00:05:20,452
First, most of the energy
escaped to the can and the air,

77
00:05:20,492 --> 00:05:24,028
and some food probably
didn't burn completely.

78
00:05:24,068 --> 00:05:29,080
Second, a label reports the energy
the body can obtain from food,

79
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:33,355
which is a different basis
from heat measured in a can.

80
00:05:33,395 --> 00:05:37,475
So call this result an
experimental water-heat yield,

81
00:05:37,515 --> 00:05:41,091
and don't treat it as
the food's label energy.

82
00:05:42,062 --> 00:05:46,344
Practice question Q zero
fifteen is worth six marks.

83
00:05:46,384 --> 00:05:48,629
Pause the video and try it.

84
00:06:04,062 --> 00:06:10,480
Ethanol burned: one hundred and two point
six four zero minus one hundred and one

85
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:15,424
point seven two zero, which is
zero point nine two zero grams.

86
00:06:15,464 --> 00:06:22,161
Divided by forty-six point zero grams per
mole: zero point zero two zero zero moles.

87
00:06:22,528 --> 00:06:27,743
Energy released by the ethanol: zero
point zero two zero zero moles times one

88
00:06:27,783 --> 00:06:33,270
thousand three hundred and seventy kilojoules
per mole is twenty-seven point four

89
00:06:33,310 --> 00:06:34,110
kilojoules.

90
00:06:34,395 --> 00:06:35,303
Now the water.

91
00:06:35,343 --> 00:06:40,383
Its temperature rise is thirty-seven
point zero minus nineteen point eight:

92
00:06:40,423 --> 00:06:42,279
seventeen point two degrees.

93
00:06:42,319 --> 00:06:47,562
The heat gained is two hundred and fifty
point zero grams times four point one

94
00:06:47,602 --> 00:06:52,777
eight times seventeen point two, which
is seventeen thousand nine hundred and

95
00:06:52,817 --> 00:06:57,314
seventy-four joules, or seventeen
point nine seven four kilojoules,

96
00:06:57,354 --> 00:06:59,115
about eighteen point zero.

97
00:06:59,462 --> 00:07:04,038
Before dividing, predict: the water
gained less than the fuel released,

98
00:07:04,078 --> 00:07:07,418
so the efficiency must be
below one hundred percent.

99
00:07:07,458 --> 00:07:10,669
Efficiency equals useful
energy over total energy:

100
00:07:10,709 --> 00:07:16,065
seventeen point nine seven four kilojoules
over twenty-seven point four kilojoules,

101
00:07:16,105 --> 00:07:20,291
times one hundred percent, which
is sixty-five point six percent.

102
00:07:20,331 --> 00:07:24,362
Both energies are in kilojoules,
so the units cancel properly.

103
00:07:24,728 --> 00:07:30,003
Marks: two for the mass and amount
of ethanol, one for the fuel energy,

104
00:07:30,043 --> 00:07:33,820
two for the water heat,
and one for the efficiency.

105
00:07:33,860 --> 00:07:34,718
Three traps.

106
00:07:34,758 --> 00:07:38,685
Using the whole burner mass
instead of the mass lost.

107
00:07:38,725 --> 00:07:43,550
Using the fuel's mass in m c delta
T instead of the water's mass.

108
00:07:43,590 --> 00:07:48,865
And dividing seventeen thousand nine
hundred and seventy-four joules by

109
00:07:48,905 --> 00:07:51,484
twenty-seven point four kilojoules,

110
00:07:51,524 --> 00:07:56,015
which gives an impossible six
hundred and fifty-six percent.

111
00:07:56,994 --> 00:08:00,892
Efficiency compares what you
get with what you put in.

112
00:08:00,932 --> 00:08:05,925
Efficiency equals useful energy
output divided by total energy input,

113
00:08:05,965 --> 00:08:07,821
times one hundred percent.

114
00:08:07,861 --> 00:08:13,258
Always say what counts as useful, and
keep both energies in the same unit.

115
00:08:13,594 --> 00:08:18,337
Sometimes you know the useful energy
you need, and the efficiency,

116
00:08:18,377 --> 00:08:20,004
and you want the input.

117
00:08:20,044 --> 00:08:25,077
Rearrange: total input equals useful
output divided by the efficiency,

118
00:08:25,117 --> 00:08:29,030
written as a fraction, like
zero point four five zero.

119
00:08:29,394 --> 00:08:31,455
Test it with limiting cases.

120
00:08:31,495 --> 00:08:37,457
At one hundred percent efficiency, the
input would just equal the useful output.

121
00:08:37,497 --> 00:08:43,534
At forty-five percent, dividing by zero
point four five zero makes the input more

122
00:08:43,574 --> 00:08:44,884
than twice as big.

123
00:08:44,924 --> 00:08:50,437
A less efficient heater needs more fuel
to deliver the same useful energy.

124
00:08:50,477 --> 00:08:54,863
Dividing by a number less than
one makes the answer larger,

125
00:08:54,903 --> 00:08:57,604
which is exactly what should happen.

126
00:08:57,994 --> 00:08:58,788
Checkpoint.

127
00:08:58,828 --> 00:09:04,403
A student finds the required input by
multiplying the useful energy by the

128
00:09:04,443 --> 00:09:05,238
efficiency.

129
00:09:05,278 --> 00:09:08,465
Will their answer be too big or too small?

130
00:09:14,927 --> 00:09:15,638
Too small.

131
00:09:15,678 --> 00:09:21,795
Multiplying by zero point four five zero
gives less than the useful output itself,

132
00:09:21,835 --> 00:09:26,075
which would mean getting more
energy out than you put in.

133
00:09:26,115 --> 00:09:27,467
That's impossible.

134
00:09:28,460 --> 00:09:32,770
Practice question Q zero
sixteen is worth five marks.

135
00:09:32,810 --> 00:09:35,026
Pause the video and try it.

136
00:09:50,460 --> 00:09:56,110
Start with what's needed: heating one
hundred and eighty point zero grams of water

137
00:09:56,150 --> 00:10:00,344
from eighteen point zero to
seventy-eight point zero degrees,

138
00:10:00,384 --> 00:10:02,773
a rise of sixty point zero degrees.

139
00:10:02,813 --> 00:10:08,256
Useful heat equals one hundred and eighty
point zero times four point one eight

140
00:10:08,296 --> 00:10:13,669
times sixty point zero: forty-five thousand
one hundred and forty-four joules,

141
00:10:13,709 --> 00:10:17,249
which is forty-five point
one four four kilojoules.

142
00:10:17,593 --> 00:10:21,403
The heater is forty-five
point zero percent efficient,

143
00:10:21,443 --> 00:10:24,539
so the chemical energy
input must be larger:

144
00:10:24,579 --> 00:10:29,387
forty-five point one four four
divided by zero point four five zero,

145
00:10:29,427 --> 00:10:32,849
which is one hundred point
three two kilojoules.

146
00:10:33,193 --> 00:10:37,529
The fuel supplies twenty-nine
point eight kilojoules per gram,

147
00:10:37,569 --> 00:10:43,035
so the mass needed is one hundred point
three two divided by twenty-nine point

148
00:10:43,075 --> 00:10:48,863
eight: three point three six six grams,
reported as three point three seven grams.

149
00:10:49,260 --> 00:10:55,059
Now the student's method: multiplying
forty-five point one four four by zero point

150
00:10:55,099 --> 00:10:59,118
four five zero, then dividing
by twenty-nine point eight,

151
00:10:59,158 --> 00:11:01,753
gives zero point six eight two grams.

152
00:11:01,793 --> 00:11:07,450
Even a perfect, one hundred percent
efficient heater would need forty-five point

153
00:11:07,490 --> 00:11:13,147
one four four divided by twenty-nine point
eight, one point five one five grams.

154
00:11:13,187 --> 00:11:16,566
So zero point six eight
two grams is impossible:

155
00:11:16,606 --> 00:11:19,953
the efficiency was used
in the wrong direction.

156
00:11:20,326 --> 00:11:25,570
Marks: two for the useful heat,
one for dividing by the efficiency,

157
00:11:25,610 --> 00:11:31,445
one for the fuel mass, and one for the
explanation with that sanity check.

158
00:11:32,393 --> 00:11:33,056
To recap.

159
00:11:33,096 --> 00:11:38,141
The fuel's energy comes from the
mass burned, converted to moles,

160
00:11:38,181 --> 00:11:41,191
times the molar enthalpy of combustion.

161
00:11:41,231 --> 00:11:46,197
The useful energy comes from q
equals m c delta T for the water.

162
00:11:46,237 --> 00:11:52,220
Efficiency is useful over total, and to
find an input from a required output,

163
00:11:52,260 --> 00:11:54,216
divide by the efficiency.

164
00:11:54,593 --> 00:11:55,689
Closing recall.

165
00:11:55,729 --> 00:12:01,673
If a heater's efficiency halved, how
would the mass of fuel needed for the same

166
00:12:01,713 --> 00:12:03,228
heating task change?

167
00:12:11,659 --> 00:12:17,400
It would double, because the required
input is the useful output divided by the

168
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:18,205
efficiency.

169
00:12:18,245 --> 00:12:23,180
Next episode: why a real calorimeter
heats more than just its water,

170
00:12:23,220 --> 00:12:25,854
and how calibration accounts for it.
